  "prompt": "[system]\n\n    You generate compact, robust Python TSP metaheuristics.\n    You must follow the requested interface exactly, always preserve tour validity.\n    \n\n[user]\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000008: cheapest_insertion\n\nStatus: valid; distance: 9.02636\n\nThought: Construct tour by cheapest-insertion heuristic: start with two farthest nodes, iteratively insert the node that incurs the smallest increase in tour length. After building the full tour, report it as best.\n\nHistory source: yes\nHistory bucket: tsp:size:100\nHistory role: regime specialist\n\nCode:\n\n```python\n\nimport numpy as np\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    n = len(distance_matrix)\n    if n <= 2:\n        tour = np.arange(n)\n        report_best_tour(tour)\n        return tour\n    start = 0\n    end = np.argmax(distance_matrix[start])\n    tour = [start, end]\n    in_tour = {start, end}\n    while len(tour) < n:\n        best_candidate = -1\n        best_increase = float('inf')\n        best_pos = -1\n        for node in range(n):\n            if node in in_tour:\n                continue\n            min_increase = float('inf')\n            min_pos = -1\n            for i in range(len(tour)):\n                a = tour[i]\n                b = tour[(i+1)%len(tour)]\n                inc = distance_matrix[a][node] + distance_matrix[node][b] - distance_matrix[a][b]\n                if inc < min_increase:\n                    min_increase = inc\n                    min_pos = i+1\n            if min_increase < best_increase:\n                best_increase = min_increase\n                best_candidate = node\n                best_pos = min_pos\n        tour.insert(best_pos, best_candidate)\n        in_tour.add(best_candidate)\n    tour_arr = np.array(tour)\n    report_best_tour(tour_arr)\n    return tour_arr\n\n```\n\nVERBAL GRADIENT: follow the reflection below as the primary source of change.\n\nUse the supplied parents as supporting evidence only.\n\nPARENT-SPECIFIC LLM REFLECTIONS:\n\nParent cand_000008 LLM reflection:\n- Summary: Cheapest-insertion starting from two farthest nodes produced 8% longer tours than farthest-insertion parent (9.03 vs 8.35) but remained fast and fully valid.\n- Aim: Reduce average tour length toward or below the parent's distance while preserving validity, speed, and timeout robustness.\n- Preserve: valid permutation construction; fast runtime (~0.23s/instance); insertion-based incremental building; report_best_tour at the end\n- Change: Replace cheapest-insertion with farthest-insertion (as in parent) to select node farthest from current tour; Add a lightweight 2-opt local search after construction to improve tour length\n- Avoid: introducing invalid tours or repeated nodes; excessive runtime that risks timeout; over-complicating the heuristic with unproven mechanisms\n\nDistance is the search objective for TSP; lower distance is better.\n\nImplement a complete TSP metaheuristic inside this required entrypoint:\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n\nRequirements:\n- Minimize total TSP tour distance.\n- Return a 1D np.ndarray containing each node exactly once.\n- Do not repeat the start node at the end.\n- Always return a valid tour.\n- Use only distance_matrix; do not assume coordinates.\n- Create a valid tour early.\n- Call report_best_tour(tour) whenever a new best valid tour is found, assume this func already exists.\n- Do not use files, network, subprocesses, or external solvers.\n- Keep the code compact and robust.\n\ncorrect signature, valid tour on every return path,\nearly report_best_tour, allowed imports only, no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"short_snake_case_name\",\n  \"thought\": \"high-level summary of the construction, improvement, and tie-break logic\",\n  \"code\": \"complete Python code as a JSON string\"\n}\nNo Markdown, fences, or text outside JSON. The code string must define solve_tsp.",
